/* TARA Critical Thinking: hand-written items in the style of the test, one bank per official question type.
   Every item: l = difficulty 1 to 3, p = passage, q = question (else the standard one of the type),
   o = five options, a = index of the right one, e = explanation in German (refers to options by content,
   because the order is shuffled). Original texts; no item is taken from an official paper. */
(function () {
  "use strict";
  const LW = window.LW;
  LW.taraItems = LW.taraItems || {};
  const B = LW.taraItems;

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Identifying the Main Conclusion
  B.conclusion = [
    { l: 1, p: "Many people assume that the best way to learn a language is to live in a country where it is spoken. But plenty of people live abroad for years without becoming fluent, because they spend their time with others who share their first language. What matters is not where you live but how much you actually use the language. So someone who practises every day at home can make faster progress than someone who lives abroad but rarely speaks the local language.",
      o: ["Living abroad is not a good way to learn a language.", "Many people live abroad for years without becoming fluent.", "What matters for learning a language is how much you use it.", "Someone who practises every day at home can make faster progress than someone abroad who rarely speaks the language.", "People who live abroad mostly spend their time with speakers of their own language."], a: 3,
      e: "Das »So« im letzten Satz leitet die Folgerung ein, die alles andere stützt. Der Satz über die Nutzung der Sprache ist ein **Zwischenschluss**: Er begründet die Hauptaussage, nicht umgekehrt. Die Aussage, Auslandsaufenthalte seien kein guter Weg, geht weiter als der Text; die Beobachtungen über Menschen im Ausland sind Gründe." },
    { l: 2, p: "The council plans to replace the town's ageing library with a smaller building and put most of its books online. Supporters point out that loans have fallen by a third in ten years. But a library is not just a store of books. It is one of the few warm, quiet public spaces where people can spend time without paying, and it is used heavily by students without space to work at home and by older residents who would otherwise be isolated. Falling loans tell us little about these uses. The council should therefore keep a building of the present size.",
      o: ["Loans from the town's library have fallen by a third in ten years.", "A library is more than a store of books.", "The council should keep a library building of the present size.", "Falling loans tell us little about how the library is used.", "Students and older residents use the library heavily."], a: 2,
      e: "»Therefore« markiert die Hauptaussage: das Gebäude in der jetzigen Größe behalten. Dass Ausleihen wenig über die Nutzung sagen, ist ein **Zwischenschluss**, der diese Forderung stützt. Der Rückgang der Ausleihen ist das Argument der Gegenseite, die übrigen Optionen sind Gründe." },
    { l: 2, p: "Schools should stop setting homework for children under eleven. Studies of primary pupils have found almost no link between the amount of homework set and how well children do in tests. Homework also causes arguments in many families, and it takes up time that young children could spend playing, reading for pleasure or sleeping, all of which are known to help them learn.",
      o: ["There is almost no link between homework and test results for primary pupils.", "Homework causes arguments in many families.", "Schools should stop setting homework for children under eleven.", "Play, reading and sleep help children learn more than homework does.", "Homework is harmful to children of all ages."], a: 2,
      e: "Die Hauptaussage steht hier **am Anfang**; alles danach begründet sie. Der Vergleich »mehr als Hausaufgaben« steht so nicht im Text, und »alle Altersstufen« ist weiter gefasst als die Forderung für Kinder unter elf." },
    { l: 3, p: "It is often said that higher taxes on sugary drinks unfairly punish the poor, since poorer households spend a larger share of their income on such drinks. Yet this objection overlooks who bears the health costs. Diseases linked to sugar are far more common in poorer communities, so the people who pay most of the tax are also those who stand to gain most from drinking less. A sugar tax may take a little more from poorer households in the short term, but it is not unfair to them once its benefits are counted.",
      o: ["Poorer households spend a larger share of their income on sugary drinks.", "A sugar tax is not unfair to poorer households once its benefits are counted.", "Diseases linked to sugar are far more common in poorer communities.", "The people who pay most of a sugar tax stand to gain most from it.", "Higher taxes on sugary drinks should be introduced."], a: 1,
      e: "Der Text weist einen Einwand zurück; die Hauptaussage ist die Zurückweisung: Die Steuer ist **nicht unfair**, wenn man den Nutzen mitzählt. Dass die Zahlenden am meisten gewinnen, ist der Zwischenschluss davor. Eine Forderung, die Steuer einzuführen, stellt der Text nicht auf; das wäre ein Schritt weiter." },
    { l: 1, p: "Our town's main road is congested every weekday morning. Most of the traffic consists of parents driving children to the two primary schools, which are less than a mile apart. If the schools organised supervised walking groups, many of these car journeys would no longer be needed. Walking groups would therefore be a cheap way to reduce the morning congestion.",
      o: ["The main road is congested every weekday morning.", "Most of the morning traffic consists of parents driving to school.", "Walking groups would be a cheap way to reduce the morning congestion.", "Parents should stop driving their children to school.", "The two primary schools are less than a mile apart."], a: 2,
      e: "»Therefore« führt zur Hauptaussage über die Laufgruppen. Die Forderung, Eltern sollten gar nicht mehr fahren, steht nicht im Text; die anderen Optionen sind Gründe oder Hintergrund." },
    { l: 3, p: "Space exploration is frequently criticised as a waste of money that could be spent solving problems on Earth. This criticism is mistaken. The budgets of space agencies are tiny compared with what governments spend on health or defence, and much of the technology developed for space, from water purification to medical imaging, has been put to use on Earth. Even if the money were redirected, there is no guarantee that it would be spent on the problems critics have in mind.",
      o: ["Space exploration is not a waste of money that would be better spent on Earth.", "Space agencies' budgets are small compared with spending on health or defence.", "Technology developed for space has been put to use on Earth.", "Money taken from space exploration would not be spent on problems on Earth.", "Governments should spend more on space exploration."], a: 0,
      e: "Die Hauptaussage ist der kurze zweite Satz: **Die Kritik ist falsch.** Alles Weitere stützt ihn. Dass umgeleitetes Geld nicht für Probleme auf der Erde ausgegeben würde, ist stärker als »keine Garantie«; mehr Geld für Raumfahrt fordert der Text nicht." },
    { l: 2, p: "Some employers now ask applicants to complete unpaid tasks, such as writing a marketing plan, as part of the selection process. They argue that this shows how candidates would really perform. But a candidate who has a job, or children to look after, may not have the hours needed to do such tasks well, while a candidate with free time will. The tasks therefore favour those with time to spare rather than those best suited to the job. Employers who want to find the strongest candidates should limit such tasks to what can be done in an hour or two.",
      o: ["Unpaid tasks show how candidates would really perform.", "Unpaid tasks favour candidates with time to spare.", "Candidates with jobs or children may lack the time to do unpaid tasks well.", "Employers who want the strongest candidates should limit unpaid tasks to an hour or two.", "Employers should not ask applicants to do unpaid tasks."], a: 3,
      e: "Der Text endet mit einer Empfehlung, die aus dem Zwischenschluss folgt: Die Aufgaben bevorzugen Menschen mit freier Zeit, **also** sollten sie kurz bleiben. Ein völliges Verbot ist stärker als die Empfehlung; die erste Option ist die Position der Arbeitgeber." },
    { l: 2, p: "Museums that charge for entry often argue that the fee is needed to maintain their collections. However, when several museums in one city stopped charging, their visitor numbers rose sharply, and the extra visitors spent money in their shops and cafés. Charging may raise money in the short run, but free entry can bring in comparable income while opening the collections to many more people. Museums should not assume that entry fees are the best way to fund their work.",
      o: ["Entry fees are needed to maintain museum collections.", "Free entry can bring in income comparable to charging.", "Museums should not assume that entry fees are the best way to fund their work.", "Museums should stop charging for entry.", "Visitor numbers rise sharply whenever museums stop charging."], a: 2,
      e: "Der letzte Satz ist die Hauptaussage, vorsichtig formuliert: Museen sollen **nicht annehmen**, dass Eintritt der beste Weg ist. Die Forderung, Eintritt abzuschaffen, geht darüber hinaus; »immer« bei den Besucherzahlen verallgemeinert ein Beispiel." },
    { l: 1, p: "Reading on screens is not necessarily worse than reading on paper. In several studies, people who read short texts on tablets understood them just as well as people who read the same texts in print. The differences that have been found mostly concern long, complex texts, and even these shrink when readers are given time to get used to the device.",
      o: ["People understand short texts equally well on tablets and on paper.", "Reading on screens is not necessarily worse than reading on paper.", "Screens are worse than paper for long, complex texts.", "Readers need time to get used to a new device.", "People should read long texts on paper."], a: 1,
      e: "Die These steht am Anfang; die Studien und die Einschränkung zu langen Texten stützen sie. Dass Bildschirme bei langen Texten schlechter seien, behauptet der Text so nicht, er sagt nur, dort seien Unterschiede gefunden worden." },
    { l: 3, p: "Critics of online courses say that students learn less without a teacher in the room. The evidence does not support so general a claim. Students who are already good at organising their own time do about as well online as in classrooms. It is students who struggle with self-discipline who fall behind, and they fall behind mainly because no one notices when they stop working. Online courses need not be worse, then, provided someone checks regularly on students who are falling behind.",
      o: ["Students learn less without a teacher in the room.", "Online courses need not be worse, provided someone checks regularly on students who fall behind.", "Students who organise their own time well do as well online as in classrooms.", "Students who struggle with self-discipline fall behind online.", "Online courses are as good as classroom teaching for every student."], a: 1,
      e: "»Then« zieht die Summe: Online-Kurse müssen **unter einer Bedingung** nicht schlechter sein. Die Bedingung gehört zur Hauptaussage. »Für jeden Studenten gleich gut« ist zu stark; die mittleren Optionen sind Belege." },
    { l: 2, p: "Cities that ban cars from their centres are often warned that shops will lose customers. In fact the opposite tends to happen. People on foot pass more shop windows and stop more often than people in cars, and a quieter street is a more pleasant place to linger. Shopkeepers who fear a ban are judging by the customers they can see parking outside, not by the many more who would come on foot.",
      o: ["Cities should ban cars from their centres.", "Shops in car-free centres tend not to lose customers, contrary to the warnings.", "People on foot stop at shops more often than people in cars.", "Shopkeepers misjudge where their customers come from.", "A quieter street is a more pleasant place to linger."], a: 1,
      e: "»In fact the opposite tends to happen« ist die Hauptaussage: Die Warnung ist falsch, Geschäfte verlieren eher keine Kunden. Die Forderung nach einem Verbot zieht der Text nicht; die Aussage über die Ladenbesitzer erklärt nur, warum sie sich irren." },
    { l: 3, p: "A four-day working week is often presented as a reward that only rich economies can afford. Yet in trials where firms cut hours without cutting pay, output usually stayed the same, because tired staff waste much of the fifth day anyway. Firms also saved on sick pay and recruitment. If output does not fall and costs do, a shorter week is not a luxury at all but a way for firms to save money.",
      o: ["Only rich economies can afford a four-day working week.", "In trials of a four-day week, output usually stayed the same.", "A shorter working week can save firms money rather than being a luxury.", "Tired staff waste much of the fifth working day.", "All firms should move to a four-day week."], a: 2,
      e: "Der letzte Satz zieht die Folgerung aus den Studienbefunden: kein Luxus, sondern eine Ersparnis. Die Belege (gleiche Leistung, geringere Kosten) stehen davor. Dass **alle** Firmen umstellen sollten, behauptet der Text nicht." },
  ];

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Drawing a Conclusion
  B.drawing = [
    { l: 1, p: "In the county cycling race, every rider who finished in the top ten had trained at altitude in the months before the race. Some riders who trained at altitude did not finish the race at all.",
      o: ["Training at altitude guarantees a top-ten finish.", "No rider in the top ten had skipped training at altitude.", "Most riders who trained at altitude finished in the top ten.", "Riders who did not train at altitude did not finish the race.", "Training at altitude made some riders too tired to finish."], a: 1,
      e: "»Jeder unter den ersten zehn hat in der Höhe trainiert« heißt dasselbe wie »**keiner** unter den ersten zehn hat darauf verzichtet«. Eine Garantie widerlegt der zweite Satz sogar; über die Mehrheit, über Fahrer ohne Höhentraining und über Gründe für Ausfälle sagt der Text nichts." },
    { l: 2, p: "At Hillside School, every student who takes Latin also takes History. No student who takes History also takes Drama. Some students who take Music take Latin.",
      o: ["No student who takes Latin takes Drama.", "Some students who take Drama take Music.", "Every student who takes History takes Latin.", "No student who takes Music takes Drama.", "Some students who take History do not take Music."], a: 0,
      e: "Latein führt zu Geschichte, Geschichte schließt Theater aus: also **kein Lateinschüler im Theaterkurs**. Musikschüler ohne Latein könnten Theater haben, deshalb folgt die Aussage über Musik und Theater nicht. Die Umkehrung »jeder Geschichtsschüler hat Latein« ist ein typischer Fehler." },
    { l: 2, p: "A bakery sells only three kinds of loaf: white, rye and spelt. Yesterday it sold more white loaves than rye loaves, and more spelt loaves than white loaves. It sold 90 loaves in total.",
      o: ["The bakery sold more than 30 spelt loaves.", "The bakery sold fewer than 30 white loaves.", "The bakery sold more than 20 rye loaves.", "Spelt loaves made up more than half of the sales.", "The bakery sold at least 10 more spelt loaves than rye loaves."], a: 0,
      e: "Dinkel ist die größte von drei Zahlen mit der Summe 90. Wären es höchstens 30, lägen die anderen beiden **darunter** und die Summe unter 90. Also mehr als 30. Gegenbeispiele für die anderen Optionen: 40 Dinkel, 31 Weiß, 19 Roggen (Weiß über 30, Roggen unter 20, Dinkel unter der Hälfte) und 31, 30, 29 (nur 2 Dinkel mehr als Roggen)." },
    { l: 1, p: "Every flight from Westport to the islands stops at Carrow. No flight that stops at Carrow arrives before noon. The 7:15 departure from Westport is a flight to the islands.",
      o: ["The 7:15 departure from Westport will not arrive before noon.", "Every flight that stops at Carrow goes to the islands.", "The 7:15 departure is the earliest flight to the islands.", "No flight from Westport arrives before noon.", "Flights to the islands take more than four hours."], a: 0,
      e: "Die Kette: Flug zu den Inseln, also Halt in Carrow, also keine Ankunft vor Mittag. Die Umkehrung »jeder Carrow-Flug geht zu den Inseln« folgt nicht, und über andere Flüge aus Westport sagt der Text nichts." },
    { l: 3, p: "In a survey of a town's households, 60% owned a car and 45% owned a bicycle. Every household that owned neither had at least one member over 70.",
      o: ["At least 5% of the households owned both a car and a bicycle.", "Most households that owned a bicycle also owned a car.", "At most 40% of the households had a member over 70.", "No household with a member over 70 owned a car.", "Exactly 5% of the households owned neither a car nor a bicycle."], a: 0,
      e: "60 % plus 45 % sind 105 %: Mindestens **5 %** müssen beides haben, sonst kämen mehr als 100 % heraus. Von 45 % Fahrradhaushalten könnten nur 5 % auch ein Auto haben, das ist keine Mehrheit. Über Haushalte mit älteren Mitgliedern lässt sich nur sagen, dass alle ohne Auto und Rad dazugehören." },
    { l: 2, p: "The library will extend its opening hours only if the council increases its grant. The council will increase the grant only if the library's visitor numbers rise this year.",
      o: ["If visitor numbers rise this year, the library will extend its opening hours.", "If the library extends its opening hours, visitor numbers will have risen this year.", "If the council increases its grant, the library will extend its opening hours.", "The library may extend its hours even if visitor numbers do not rise.", "The council will increase the grant this year."], a: 1,
      e: "»Nur wenn« nennt **notwendige** Bedingungen: Längere Öffnung setzt den Zuschuss voraus, der Zuschuss steigende Besucherzahlen. Rückwärts gelesen: Wird verlängert, sind die Zahlen gestiegen. Hinreichend ist keine der Bedingungen, deshalb folgen die Wenn-dann-Sätze in Vorwärtsrichtung nicht." },
    { l: 1, p: "All of the tomatoes in the greenhouse were planted in March. Some of the tomatoes in the greenhouse are of the 'Sungold' variety.",
      o: ["Some 'Sungold' tomatoes were planted in March.", "All 'Sungold' tomatoes were planted in March.", "All tomatoes planted in March are in the greenhouse.", "Most of the tomatoes in the greenhouse are 'Sungold'.", "No tomatoes outside the greenhouse were planted in March."], a: 0,
      e: "Die Sungold-Tomaten im Gewächshaus sind im März gepflanzt, also **einige** Sungold-Tomaten. Über Sungold außerhalb und über andere Märzpflanzungen sagt der Text nichts." },
    { l: 3, p: "A train leaves Ashby every 40 minutes, starting at 06:00. A bus leaves Ashby every 25 minutes, also starting at 06:00. Both services run until midnight.",
      o: ["A train and a bus leave Ashby together at 09:20.", "A train and a bus leave Ashby together every hour.", "A bus leaves Ashby at 08:00.", "More trains than buses leave Ashby each day.", "A train and a bus leave Ashby together at 07:40."], a: 0,
      e: "Gemeinsam fahren sie im Abstand des kleinsten gemeinsamen Vielfachen von 40 und 25 Minuten: **200 Minuten**, also 3 Stunden 20 Minuten nach 06:00, um 09:20. Busse um 08:00 gibt es nicht (120 ist kein Vielfaches von 25), und Busse fahren öfter als Züge." },
    { l: 2, p: "Every candidate who scored above 80 in the written test was invited to interview. Only candidates who were invited to interview and who held a driving licence were offered the job. Sam was offered the job.",
      o: ["Sam scored above 80 in the written test.", "Sam holds a driving licence.", "Everyone with a driving licence who was interviewed was offered the job.", "Sam was the only candidate offered the job.", "Candidates who scored 80 or below were not interviewed."], a: 1,
      e: "Ein Angebot setzt Einladung **und** Führerschein voraus, also hat Sam einen Führerschein. Ob Sam über 80 lag, bleibt offen: Über 80 führt zur Einladung, aber eine Einladung kann auch andere Gründe haben." },
    { l: 3, p: "In a quiz, each correct answer scores 3 points and each wrong answer loses 1 point; a question left unanswered scores nothing. Priya answered all 20 questions and scored 32 points.",
      o: ["Priya answered 13 questions correctly.", "Priya answered more than 15 questions correctly.", "Priya got 8 answers wrong.", "Priya would have scored more than 40 points by leaving her wrong answers blank.", "Priya scored more than two thirds of the maximum possible score."], a: 0,
      e: "Mit $r$ richtigen gilt $3r - (20 - r) = 32$, also $4r = 52$ und $r = 13$. Falsch waren 7; ohne sie hätte sie 39 Punkte. Zwei Drittel von 60 wären 40." },
    { l: 2, p: "Everyone who sings in the Hartfield choir also plays in the town band. Some members of the town band are under eighteen. Nobody under eighteen is allowed to travel on the band's foreign tour.",
      o: ["Some members of the choir cannot go on the foreign tour.", "Some members of the town band cannot go on the foreign tour.", "Nobody in the choir is under eighteen.", "Every member of the band sings in the choir.", "Everyone over eighteen in the band will go on the tour."], a: 1,
      e: "Einige Bandmitglieder sind unter 18, und unter 18 darf niemand mit: also können **einige Bandmitglieder** nicht mit. Ob die Minderjährigen auch im Chor singen, weiß man nicht; »dürfen« heißt auch nicht, dass alle Älteren fahren." },
  ];

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Identifying an Assumption
  B.assumption = [
    { l: 1, p: "The new bypass will reduce traffic in the town centre, since lorries heading for the port will no longer need to drive through it. This means that the centre's air will be cleaner within a year of the bypass opening.",
      o: ["Lorries heading for the port contribute to air pollution in the town centre.", "The bypass will open within a year.", "Cars cause more pollution than lorries.", "The port will become busier after the bypass opens.", "Traffic is the only cause of air pollution in the town centre."], a: 0,
      e: "**Verneinungstest:** Trügen die Lkw nichts zur Luftverschmutzung bei, würde ihr Wegfall die Luft nicht sauberer machen; das Argument bräche zusammen. Dass Verkehr die **einzige** Ursache sei, braucht es nicht, das ist zu stark." },
    { l: 2, p: "Sales of printed newspapers have fallen every year for the last fifteen years. Yet the number of people reading news has never been higher. So newspapers will survive only if they can persuade readers to pay for news online.",
      o: ["Most people now read news online rather than in print.", "Newspapers cannot cover their costs online through advertising alone.", "Readers are already willing to pay for news online.", "The fall in printed sales will continue for another fifteen years.", "People read more news now than fifteen years ago."], a: 1,
      e: "Die Folgerung »nur wenn Leser zahlen« setzt voraus, dass es **keinen anderen Weg** gibt, online die Kosten zu decken, vor allem Werbung. Ob Leser schon zahlen wollen, ist nicht nötig, der Text sagt ja »wenn sie sie überzeugen können«; die letzte Option steht schon als Grund im Text." },
    { l: 2, p: "Pupils who eat breakfast do better in morning lessons than those who do not. So if the school provided free breakfasts, the results of its pupils would improve.",
      o: ["Eating breakfast causes better performance in morning lessons rather than merely going along with it.", "Every pupil would take up the offer of a free breakfast.", "Pupils who skip breakfast do so because their families cannot afford it.", "Morning lessons matter more than afternoon lessons.", "The school can afford to provide free breakfasts."], a: 0,
      e: "Aus einem **Zusammenhang** wird eine Wirkung gemacht. Wären die Frühstückenden aus anderen Gründen besser (etwa ruhigere Familien), brächte das Frühstück nichts. Dass **jeder** mitmacht, ist zu stark; ob die Schule es bezahlen kann, betrifft die Folgerung »die Ergebnisse würden besser« nicht." },
    { l: 3, p: "The city's tourist office reports that visitors who join a guided walking tour stay on average two nights longer than those who do not. The council should therefore subsidise walking tours, because longer stays will bring more money into the local economy.",
      o: ["Most visitors to the city have never joined a guided walking tour.", "Walking tours are more enjoyable than the city's other attractions.", "Joining a walking tour leads visitors to stay longer, rather than visitors who already plan long stays being more likely to join one.", "Visitors who stay longer are more likely to return in future.", "The tourist office collects data on every visitor to the city."], a: 2,
      e: "Der Zuschuss lohnt nur, wenn die Führung den Aufenthalt **verlängert**. Buchen einfach Langzeitgäste eher Führungen, ändert mehr Führungen nichts an der Dauer. Die anderen Optionen sind für die Folgerung nicht nötig." },
    { l: 1, p: "Ella has applied for the post of head chef. She has more experience than any other applicant, so she will be appointed.",
      o: ["The most experienced applicant will be chosen for the post.", "Ella is the best cook among the applicants.", "The other applicants have no experience at all.", "Head chefs need at least ten years' experience.", "Ella applied before the other candidates."], a: 0,
      e: "Von »am erfahrensten« zu »wird genommen« fehlt ein Schritt: Erfahrung muss **entscheiden**. Verneint man das, fällt die Folgerung. Dass die anderen gar keine Erfahrung haben, ist zu stark." },
    { l: 2, p: "Bird numbers in the valley have fallen since the new wind turbines were built on the ridge. The turbines must be killing the birds, so they should be switched off during the spring migration.",
      o: ["Nothing else that changed in the valley at the same time explains the fall in bird numbers.", "Most birds killed by turbines die during the spring migration.", "Wind turbines are the main cause of bird deaths across the country.", "The turbines produce little electricity in spring.", "Bird numbers in the neighbouring valleys have not fallen."], a: 0,
      e: "»Müssen die Vögel töten« schließt **andere Ursachen** stillschweigend aus. Gäbe es eine andere Erklärung, wäre der Schluss nicht gedeckt. »Die meisten« Todesfälle im Frühjahr ist zu stark, und stabile Zahlen in Nachbartälern würden stützen, sind aber nicht nötig." },
    { l: 3, p: "Students at Greyfield College who attend the optional revision classes get better exam grades than those who do not. The college wants to raise its exam grades, so it should make the revision classes compulsory.",
      o: ["Students who would attend only because they were made to would also benefit from the classes.", "Most students at the college currently skip the revision classes.", "The revision classes are taught by the college's best teachers.", "Making the classes compulsory would cost the college nothing.", "Exam grades at Greyfield are lower than at other colleges."], a: 0,
      e: "Freiwillige sind vermutlich motivierter. Pflicht hilft nur, wenn **auch die bisher Fehlenden** davon profitieren würden. Verneint man das, bringt die Pflicht nichts. Die übrigen Optionen sind für die Folgerung nicht nötig." },
    { l: 2, p: "Fewer people are buying music on CDs, but sales of vinyl records have risen for ten years in a row. This shows that people still value owning music in physical form.",
      o: ["People who buy vinyl records do so at least partly because they want to own music in physical form.", "Vinyl records sound better than CDs.", "Sales of vinyl now exceed sales of CDs.", "Most people who used to buy CDs now buy vinyl.", "Streaming services will soon lose subscribers."], a: 0,
      e: "Aus steigenden Verkäufen auf das **Motiv** zu schließen, setzt voraus, dass dieses Motiv wenigstens teilweise dahintersteht. Kauften alle Schallplatten nur als Deko oder Geldanlage, sagte der Trend nichts über den Wert physischer Musik." },
    { l: 1, p: "This year's school play sold out on all three nights. Next year the school should put on four performances, since that would bring in more money.",
      o: ["There would be enough demand next year to sell tickets for a fourth performance.", "Next year's play will be better than this year's.", "Ticket prices should be raised next year.", "Every member of the audience came on more than one night.", "The school hall could hold more people."], a: 0,
      e: "Ausverkauft heißt nicht, dass für eine **vierte** Vorstellung genug Interessierte da sind. Ohne diese Nachfrage gäbe es kein zusätzliches Geld. Ob das Stück besser wird, spielt für den Schluss keine Rolle." },
    { l: 3, p: "Countries with more doctors per person tend to have longer life expectancy. So the most effective way for a country to increase life expectancy is to train more doctors.",
      o: ["Training more doctors would raise life expectancy more than any other measure a country could take.", "Doctors are spread evenly across each country.", "Rich countries have more doctors than poor countries.", "Life expectancy is the best measure of a country's health.", "Countries with few doctors have short life expectancy."], a: 0,
      e: "»Der wirksamste Weg« ist ein Vergleich mit **allen anderen** Maßnahmen, über die der Text nichts sagt. Ohne diese Annahme folgt höchstens, dass mehr Ärzte helfen könnten. Dass reiche Länder mehr Ärzte haben, ist eine mögliche Störgröße, keine Annahme des Arguments." },
    { l: 2, p: "Since the canteen started labelling the calories of each dish, sales of the high-calorie dishes have fallen by a fifth. The labels have therefore made staff eat more healthily.",
      o: ["Staff did not simply buy the high-calorie dishes elsewhere instead.", "Calorie labels are required by law.", "The canteen's low-calorie dishes are cheaper.", "Every member of staff eats in the canteen every day.", "High-calorie dishes are always unhealthy."], a: 0,
      e: "Dass in der Kantine weniger Kalorienreiches verkauft wird, zeigt nur dann gesünderes Essen, wenn die Leute es **nicht einfach woanders** kaufen. Verneint man das, bleibt die Ernährung gleich. »Immer ungesund« und »jeden Tag« sind zu stark." },
  ];

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Assessing the Impact of Additional Evidence
  const W = "Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the above argument?";
  const S = "Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the above argument?";
  B.evidence = [
    { l: 1, q: W, p: "Since the town introduced a 20 mph speed limit in residential streets, the number of road accidents in those streets has fallen by a quarter. The lower limit has clearly made the streets safer.",
      o: ["In the same period, accidents fell by a quarter in nearby towns that kept the old limit.", "Many drivers dislike the new speed limit.", "Accidents at 20 mph are less likely to cause serious injuries.", "The town also put up new signs showing the speed limit.", "Most residents supported the new limit."], a: 0,
      e: "Fiel die Zahl **auch ohne** Tempolimit genauso, war wohl etwas anderes die Ursache: Das ist eine Vergleichsgruppe, die die Wirkung infrage stellt. Weniger schwere Verletzungen stützen eher; Meinungen über das Limit sagen nichts über die Sicherheit." },
    { l: 2, q: S, p: "A café owner noticed that on days when she played slow music, customers stayed longer and spent more. She concluded that slow music causes customers to spend more.",
      o: ["When she switched between slow and fast music at random, spending was still higher on slow-music days.", "Slow-music days were mostly at weekends, when customers have more time.", "Many customers said they did not notice the music.", "Other cafés in the town play fast music.", "The café's prices are higher than those of nearby cafés."], a: 0,
      e: "Ein **zufälliger** Wechsel schließt aus, dass etwas anderes (Wochentag, Wetter) die Musik begleitet. Bleibt der Effekt, ist die Ursache plausibler. Dass langsame Musik vor allem am Wochenende lief, schwächt den Schluss." },
    { l: 2, q: W, p: "In a survey of 2,000 readers of a fitness magazine, 70% said they exercise at least four times a week. The magazine concluded that most adults in the country exercise regularly.",
      o: ["Readers of fitness magazines are much more likely to exercise than adults in general.", "The survey was carried out online.", "A sample of 2,000 people is large.", "Some respondents exercise more than once a day.", "The magazine has been published for twenty years."], a: 0,
      e: "Die Stichprobe ist **nicht repräsentativ**: Leser eines Fitnessmagazins treiben eher Sport als der Durchschnitt. Die Größe der Stichprobe hilft dagegen nicht; sie würde eher stützen." },
    { l: 3, q: W, p: "In the last five years the city has built 3,000 new flats, yet average rents have continued to rise. Building more housing evidently does not make rents more affordable.",
      o: ["Without the new flats, rents would have risen much faster, because the city's population grew by 20,000 in the same period.", "Most of the new flats were built on former industrial land.", "Rents have also risen in other cities.", "Some of the new flats are still empty.", "Building costs have risen in the last five years."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Argument vergleicht mit dem Zustand **vorher** statt mit dem, was **ohne** Neubau passiert wäre. Wuchs die Nachfrage stark, können neue Wohnungen den Anstieg gebremst haben. Steigende Mieten auch anderswo erklären nichts über die Wirkung des Bauens." },
    { l: 2, q: S, p: "Children who learn a musical instrument tend to do better at mathematics. Learning an instrument therefore improves mathematical ability.",
      o: ["Children randomly chosen to receive music lessons improved more in mathematics over two years than a similar group who did not.", "Children who are good at mathematics are more likely to choose to learn an instrument.", "Most professional musicians did not study mathematics after school.", "Music lessons are expensive.", "Some children who learn an instrument do badly at mathematics."], a: 0,
      e: "Eine **zufällige Zuteilung** trennt Ursache und Auswahl: Wer Unterricht bekam, verbesserte sich stärker. Dass gute Mathematiker eher ein Instrument wählen, kehrt die Richtung um und schwächt; teure Stunden deuten auf das Elterneinkommen als Störgröße." },
    { l: 3, q: W, p: "Hospital A has a higher death rate among its patients than Hospital B. Patients needing an operation should therefore choose Hospital B.",
      o: ["Hospital A is the region's specialist centre and treats the most seriously ill patients.", "Hospital B has more beds than Hospital A.", "Hospital A has newer buildings than Hospital B.", "Hospital B employs more nurses than Hospital A.", "Both hospitals treat a similar number of patients each year."], a: 0,
      e: "Behandelt A die **schwersten Fälle**, ist seine höhere Sterberate kein Zeichen schlechterer Versorgung. Mehr Pflegekräfte in B würden eher stützen; Betten und Gebäude sagen nichts über die Behandlung." },
    { l: 1, q: W, p: "Last winter, sales of umbrellas in our shop fell by half. Our new umbrella design must be unpopular.",
      o: ["Last winter was the driest on record in our area.", "The new design comes in five colours.", "Our shop also sells raincoats.", "The new design costs the same as the old one.", "Some customers said they preferred the old design."], a: 0,
      e: "Ein **trockener** Winter erklärt den Rückgang ohne das Design. Kunden, die das alte Modell lieber mochten, stützen den Schluss eher." },
    { l: 2, q: S, p: "A new app that reminds people to drink water has been downloaded a million times. Its makers claim that it improves the health of its users.",
      o: ["In a trial, people given the app reported fewer headaches over three months than a similar group without it.", "The app is free to download.", "Many users stop using the app after a week.", "Most people already drink enough water.", "The app was designed by a team of programmers."], a: 0,
      e: "Downloads zeigen Beliebtheit, nicht Wirkung. Ein **Vergleich mit einer ähnlichen Gruppe** liefert Belege für die Wirkung. Dass viele schnell aufhören oder ohnehin genug trinken, schwächt die Behauptung." },
    { l: 3, q: W, p: "The proportion of graduates among the town's adults rose from 20% to 30% in ten years, while unemployment fell. Clearly, raising the number of graduates reduces unemployment.",
      o: ["In the same decade a large car factory opened in the town and hired thousands of workers without degrees.", "Graduates usually earn more than non-graduates.", "The town's university was founded fifty years ago.", "Unemployment fell faster among graduates than among non-graduates.", "The town's population grew slightly."], a: 0,
      e: "Eine **andere Ursache** im selben Zeitraum erklärt den Rückgang: Die Fabrik stellte gerade Menschen ohne Abschluss ein. Dass die Arbeitslosigkeit unter Absolventen schneller sank, passt eher zur Behauptung." },
    { l: 2, q: W, p: "Our evening pottery classes are always fully booked, so there is clearly demand for a second pottery class on Saturday mornings.",
      o: ["Most of the people who book the evening classes work on Saturdays.", "The evening class has twelve places.", "Pottery has become a popular hobby across the country.", "Some people have been on the waiting list for months.", "The pottery teacher is free on Saturdays."], a: 0,
      e: "Volle Abendkurse zeigen Nachfrage **am Abend**. Arbeiten die Interessierten samstags, nützt ihnen ein Samstagskurs nichts. Eine lange Warteliste stützt dagegen; ob die Lehrerin Zeit hat, betrifft die Nachfrage nicht." },
    { l: 3, q: S, p: "Since a supermarket chain began charging 10p for plastic bags, the number of bags it sells has fallen by 80%. The charge has reduced the amount of plastic its customers use.",
      o: ["Customers have not switched to buying more plastic bin bags or other plastic packaging instead.", "The money from the charge goes to charities.", "Other supermarket chains have introduced the same charge.", "Plastic bags take hundreds of years to break down.", "Most customers think the charge is fair."], a: 0,
      e: "Weniger verkaufte Tüten bedeuten nur dann weniger Plastik, wenn es **keine Ausweichkäufe** gibt. Schließt man sie aus, wird der Schluss stärker. Spenden, Meinungen und Zerfallszeiten ändern nichts an der Menge." },
  ];

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Detecting Reasoning Errors
  B.errors = [
    { l: 1, p: "Every successful entrepreneur I have read about left school early. So if you want to succeed in business, you should leave school early.",
      o: ["It draws a general conclusion from cases that may not be typical, since unsuccessful people who left school early are rarely written about.", "It assumes that entrepreneurs need no qualifications.", "It confuses a cause with an effect.", "It relies on the opinion of a single expert.", "It assumes that everyone wants to succeed in business."], a: 0,
      e: "Über Erfolgreiche wird geschrieben, über die vielen Gescheiterten mit derselben Entscheidung nicht. Die Fälle sind **nicht repräsentativ**, daraus folgt kein Rat. Der Text stützt sich auf Berichte, nicht auf einen einzelnen Experten." },
    { l: 2, p: "Nobody can become a doctor without a good degree in a science subject. Hannah, who wants to be a doctor, has a first-class degree in chemistry, so she will certainly become one.",
      o: ["It treats something that is needed to become a doctor as if it were enough to become one.", "It assumes that chemistry is the best science subject.", "It generalises from a single case.", "It ignores the possibility that a degree in another subject could also qualify someone.", "It attacks Hannah rather than her qualifications."], a: 0,
      e: "Ein guter Abschluss ist **notwendig**, aber nicht **hinreichend**: Das Argument behandelt die Voraussetzung wie eine Garantie. Andere Fächer spielen für den Schluss keine Rolle." },
    { l: 2, p: "Cities with more police officers per person have higher crime rates. So employing more police officers increases crime.",
      o: ["It overlooks the possibility that high crime rates lead cities to employ more police officers.", "It assumes that all crimes are reported to the police.", "It relies on figures from too few cities.", "It assumes that police officers commit crimes.", "It treats a necessary condition as a sufficient one."], a: 0,
      e: "Der Zusammenhang kann **umgekehrt** laufen: Wo viel Kriminalität ist, stellt man mehr Polizei ein. Daraus eine Wirkung der Polizei abzuleiten, ist der Fehler. Wie viele Städte untersucht wurden, sagt der Text nicht." },
    { l: 3, p: "The number of cycling accidents in Northam rose by 40% last year, while in Southam it rose by only 10%. So cycling is now more dangerous in Northam than in Southam.",
      o: ["It compares percentage increases without considering how many accidents, or how much cycling, there was in each town.", "It assumes that all cycling accidents are reported.", "It assumes that the rise in accidents will continue.", "It ignores the fact that Southam has more cyclists.", "It confuses cycling accidents with road accidents in general."], a: 0,
      e: "Prozentuale **Anstiege** sagen nichts über das **Niveau**: 40 % von 5 Unfällen sind weniger als 10 % von 100. Gefährlicher heißt außerdem: mehr Unfälle pro gefahrener Strecke. Dass Southam mehr Radfahrer hat, steht nicht im Text." },
    { l: 1, p: "Professor Lane argues that the new shopping centre will harm the small shops in the town. But Professor Lane lives in the city, not in the town, so her argument can be ignored.",
      o: ["It rejects an argument because of who made it rather than because of what it says.", "It assumes that the shopping centre will be built.", "It assumes that small shops are important to the town.", "It generalises from one professor to all experts.", "It confuses the town with the city."], a: 0,
      e: "Wo jemand wohnt, macht ein Argument nicht falsch. Der Text **greift die Person an** statt ihrer Gründe." },
    { l: 2, p: "Either the council raises parking charges or it will have to close the swimming pool. Nobody wants the pool to close, so the council must raise parking charges.",
      o: ["It assumes that raising parking charges and closing the pool are the only options.", "It assumes that everyone uses the swimming pool.", "It assumes that parking charges are too low.", "It relies on the views of people who do not use the pool.", "It assumes that the pool makes a profit."], a: 0,
      e: "Ein **falsches Entweder-oder**: Es könnte andere Wege geben, etwa Sparen an anderer Stelle oder Zuschüsse. Ohne diese Alternativen zu prüfen, folgt keine Pflicht zu höheren Gebühren." },
    { l: 3, p: "Each of the musicians in the new orchestra has won an international prize. The orchestra will therefore give outstanding performances.",
      o: ["It assumes that what is true of each member individually will be true of the group as a whole.", "It assumes that international prizes are hard to win.", "It relies on the opinions of prize judges.", "It ignores the possibility that the orchestra has too few members.", "It confuses winning a prize with deserving one."], a: 0,
      e: "Von den **Teilen** auf das **Ganze**: Ein Orchester braucht Zusammenspiel, das Einzelpreise nicht belegen." },
    { l: 2, p: "Since the new manager arrived in January, the team has lost four of its six matches. The club should replace him before the season is ruined.",
      o: ["It assumes that because the defeats followed the manager's arrival, he caused them.", "It treats a necessary condition as if it were sufficient.", "It attacks the manager personally.", "It assumes that the club can afford a new manager.", "It confuses the number of matches with the number of goals."], a: 0,
      e: "»Danach, also deswegen«: Die Niederlagen kamen **nach** seiner Ankunft, aber das zeigt nicht, dass er sie verursacht hat (Verletzungen, schwere Gegner)." },
    { l: 3, p: "This newspaper is a reliable source of information, because every article it publishes is checked for accuracy. We know that its articles are checked for accuracy because the newspaper says so on its front page.",
      o: ["It relies on the newspaper's own claim to establish that the newspaper is reliable.", "It assumes that all newspapers check their articles.", "It confuses accuracy with balance.", "It generalises from a few articles to all of them.", "It assumes that readers read the front page."], a: 0,
      e: "Ein **Zirkel**: Die Zuverlässigkeit wird mit einer Behauptung der Zeitung belegt, deren Zuverlässigkeit erst zu zeigen wäre." },
    { l: 2, p: "If the dam had failed, the valley would have flooded. The valley has flooded, so the dam must have failed.",
      o: ["It overlooks the possibility that the flooding had some other cause.", "It assumes that the dam was badly built.", "It treats a possible event as if it were certain.", "It relies on an unrepresentative example.", "It confuses the valley with the dam."], a: 0,
      e: "Aus der **Folge** auf **eine** bestimmte Ursache zu schließen, übersieht andere: Starkregen kann das Tal ebenso überfluten." },
    { l: 3, p: "Most people who win the lottery bought their ticket at a busy city-centre shop. So if you want to win the lottery, you should buy your ticket in a busy city-centre shop.",
      o: ["It ignores the fact that busy shops sell far more tickets, so more winning tickets would be bought there anyway.", "It assumes that lottery draws are fair.", "It relies on a small number of winners.", "It confuses wanting to win with being likely to win.", "It assumes that everyone can reach a city-centre shop."], a: 0,
      e: "Belebte Läden verkaufen **mehr Lose**, deshalb stammen von dort mehr Gewinne, ohne dass ein einzelnes Los dort bessere Chancen hätte. Der Schluss übersieht diese Basisrate." },
  ];

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Matching Arguments
  const M = "Which one of the following most closely parallels the reasoning used in the above argument?";
  B.matching = [
    { l: 2, q: M, p: "All the best chess players study old games. Tom studies old games, so Tom must be one of the best chess players.",
      o: ["All successful gardeners test their soil. Marta tests her soil, so Marta must be a successful gardener.", "All successful gardeners test their soil. Marta does not test her soil, so she is not a successful gardener.", "All successful gardeners test their soil. Marta is a successful gardener, so she tests her soil.", "Most successful gardeners test their soil. Marta tests her soil, so she is probably a successful gardener.", "Only successful gardeners test their soil. Marta tests her soil, so Marta is a successful gardener."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: Alle A sind B, x ist B, also ist x ein A. Das ist **derselbe Fehler** wie beim Schach. Mit »nur« oder umgekehrter Richtung wird der Schluss gültig, mit »die meisten« ändert sich die Stärke." },
    { l: 2, q: M, p: "Our restaurant's takings rose after we started opening on Sundays. So opening on Sundays has made us more money.",
      o: ["My plants grew faster after I started talking to them, so talking to them has made them grow faster.", "My plants grow faster when I water them, because water is necessary for growth.", "Plants that are talked to grow faster, so I should talk to mine.", "Most gardeners who talk to their plants say they grow faster, so it must work.", "If I talk to my plants they grow faster; they are growing faster, so I must have talked to them."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: B geschah **nach** A, also hat A B bewirkt. Die Pflanzen-Version hat genau diesen Aufbau. Die anderen stützen sich auf eine Regel, auf Meinungen oder auf einen Wenn-dann-Satz." },
    { l: 3, q: M, p: "Last year, 30 of the 120 members of the hiking club were under 25. This year the club has 200 members, so it should have about 50 members under 25.",
      o: ["Last season 12 of the 48 games the team played were away games. This season it plays 60 games, so about 15 should be away games.", "Last year 30 members were under 25. This year there are more members, so there must be more members under 25.", "Last year a quarter of the members were under 25, so this year a quarter will be over 25.", "The club grew from 120 to 200 members, so it grew by two thirds.", "Last season 12 of the 48 games were away games, so this season the team will win a quarter of its games."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: Ein **Anteil** aus dem Vorjahr (ein Viertel) wird auf eine neue Gesamtzahl übertragen. 12 von 48 sind ein Viertel, ein Viertel von 60 sind 15. Die anderen Optionen übertragen keinen Anteil oder verwechseln Größen." },
    { l: 2, q: M, p: "Either Ravi took the early train or he missed the meeting. He was at the meeting, so he took the early train.",
      o: ["Either the order was sent on Monday or it will arrive late. It has arrived on time, so it was sent on Monday.", "Either the order was sent on Monday or it will arrive late. It was sent on Monday, so it will arrive on time.", "If the order was sent on Monday, it will arrive on time. It arrived on time, so it was sent on Monday.", "The order was sent on Monday and arrived on time, so orders sent on Monday arrive on time.", "Orders sent on Monday usually arrive on time, so this one probably will."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: Entweder A oder B; nicht B; also A. Genau so beim Paket: nicht verspätet, also am Montag verschickt. Die zweite Option schließt aus A auf nicht B, das folgt bei einem einschließenden »oder« nicht." },
    { l: 2, q: M, p: "No reptile is warm-blooded. All snakes are reptiles. So no snake is warm-blooded.",
      o: ["No member of the choir is under sixteen. All the sopranos are members of the choir. So no soprano is under sixteen.", "No member of the choir is under sixteen. Some sopranos are under sixteen. So some sopranos are not members of the choir.", "All members of the choir are over sixteen. All the sopranos are over sixteen. So all the sopranos are members of the choir.", "No soprano is under sixteen, so all the sopranos are members of the choir.", "Some members of the choir are sopranos, and no soprano is under sixteen, so no member of the choir is under sixteen."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: Kein A ist B; alle C sind A; also ist kein C ein B. Die Chor-Version entspricht dem Satz für Satz. Die zweite Option ist ebenfalls gültig, hat aber einen anderen Aufbau (»einige«)." },
    { l: 3, q: M, p: "If the price of fuel rises, airlines raise their fares. Airlines have not raised their fares, so the price of fuel has not risen.",
      o: ["If a student revises, she passes the test. She did not pass the test, so she did not revise.", "If a student revises, she passes the test. She passed the test, so she revised.", "If a student revises, she passes the test. She did not revise, so she did not pass.", "A student who revises usually passes, so a student who passed probably revised.", "Students who revise pass, and she is a student, so she passes."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: Wenn A, dann B; nicht B; also nicht A (gültig). Die Prüfungs-Version entspricht genau. Die zweite und dritte Option sind die bekannten Fehlschlüsse: Folge bejahen und Bedingung verneinen." },
    { l: 3, q: M, p: "Since the shop began playing loud music, younger customers have come in more often and older customers less often. So whether customers like loud music depends on their age.",
      o: ["Since the gym added evening classes, people who work during the day have come more often and retired people less often. So whether people like evening classes depends on whether they work during the day.", "Since the gym added evening classes, more people have come. So people like evening classes.", "Since the gym added evening classes, retired people come less often, so the gym should stop them.", "People who work during the day prefer evening classes, so the gym should add more of them.", "Since the gym added evening classes, people who work during the day have come more often, so evening classes are the most popular."], a: 0,
      e: "Struktur: Nach einer Änderung kommt **eine Gruppe öfter, eine andere seltener**; daraus wird geschlossen, dass die Vorliebe von dem Merkmal abhängt, das die Gruppen trennt. Nur die erste Option bildet beide Gruppen und den Schluss auf das Merkmal ab." },
  ];

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ Applying Principles
  const P = "Which one of the following best illustrates the principle underlying the above argument?";
  B.principles = [
    { l: 2, q: P, p: "A shop that sells a product it knows to be faulty should refund the customer even if the customer has lost the receipt, because it is the shop's knowledge, not the customer's paperwork, that makes the sale unfair.",
      o: ["A landlord who knew that a flat was damp when he let it should pay for the tenant's ruined furniture, even if the tenant never reported the damp in writing.", "A customer who breaks a product should not expect a refund.", "A shop should keep copies of all receipts so that customers can claim refunds.", "A restaurant should refund a customer who did not enjoy the meal.", "A seller who did not know that a product was faulty should still refund the buyer."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: Wer **wissentlich** Mangelhaftes verkauft, muss einstehen, auch wenn dem Käufer ein Formnachweis fehlt. Der Vermieter wusste vom Schimmel, die fehlende schriftliche Meldung ändert nichts. Die letzte Option dreht den Kern um: Hier fehlt gerade das Wissen." },
    { l: 3, q: P, p: "We should not penalise students for missing a deadline if the reason they missed it was beyond their control. A student whose bus broke down on the way to hand in an essay should therefore not lose marks.",
      o: ["A worker should not lose pay for arriving late if a sudden storm closed the road to the office.", "A student who forgot the deadline should lose marks.", "A shop should refund customers whose goods arrive late.", "A team that loses because of injuries should still be relegated.", "A driver who speeds because she is late for an exam should not be fined."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: **keine Strafe** für ein Versäumnis, dessen Ursache man nicht beeinflussen konnte. Der Sturm passt genau. Wer vergisst, hatte es in der Hand; darüber sagt das Prinzip nichts. Zu schnelles Fahren ist eine eigene Entscheidung." },
    { l: 2, q: P, p: "It is wrong to read someone's diary without their permission, even if what you read would help you to protect them, because people have a right to decide who knows their private thoughts.",
      o: ["It is wrong for a doctor to tell a patient's family about the patient's illness without the patient's agreement, even if the family could then give better support.", "It is right to read a diary if its owner has died.", "Parents should be allowed to read their children's messages to keep them safe.", "People should not write private thoughts in diaries.", "It is wrong to lie to someone in order to protect them."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: Das Recht, über **private Informationen** zu bestimmen, wiegt schwerer als ein gut gemeinter Nutzen. Der Arzt-Fall hat genau diese Struktur. Die Eltern-Option widerspricht dem Prinzip; Lügen betrifft Ehrlichkeit, nicht Privatsphäre." },
    { l: 1, q: P, p: "Those who cause pollution should pay to clean it up. So the factory that leaked oil into the river should pay for the river to be cleaned.",
      o: ["A dog owner whose dog damages a neighbour's fence should pay for the repairs.", "The council should pay to clean the river because it is a public space.", "The factory should be closed because it caused pollution.", "People who swim in the river should pay for it to be cleaned.", "Everyone should pay a small tax to keep rivers clean."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: **Wer einen Schaden verursacht, bezahlt ihn.** Der Hundebesitzer haftet für den Zaun. Bei den anderen zahlen Nutzer oder die Allgemeinheit, oder es geht um Strafe statt Ersatz." },
    { l: 3, q: P, p: "A promise made under pressure need not be kept. When the shop owner agreed to sell his land only because the developer threatened to report him to the tax authorities, he was not bound by that agreement.",
      o: ["A student who agreed to lend her notes only because a classmate threatened to spread rumours about her is not obliged to hand them over.", "A person who forgets a promise should apologise.", "A contract signed by a child is not valid.", "A promise made to someone who has since died need not be kept.", "Promises should only be made after careful thought."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: **Unter Druck** gegebene Zusagen binden nicht. Die Studentin sagte nur wegen einer Drohung zu. Der Kindervertrag scheitert an der Geschäftsfähigkeit, nicht an Druck." },
    { l: 2, q: P, p: "If a rule has never been enforced and everyone knows this, it is unfair to punish someone for breaking it without first announcing that it will be enforced. So the library should not fine readers for eating in the reading room without giving notice first.",
      o: ["A park that has never enforced its ban on cycling should announce that it will do so before fining cyclists.", "A library should not have rules about eating.", "People who break rules should always be punished.", "A shop that has always allowed returns should continue to do so.", "A rule that is unfair should not be enforced."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: Eine **nie durchgesetzte** Regel darf man erst nach einer Ankündigung bestrafen. Der Park mit dem Radverbot passt genau. Die Rückgabe-Option betrifft Beständigkeit einer Kulanz, nicht die Ankündigung vor einer Strafe." },
    { l: 2, q: P, p: "We should judge a scientific claim by the evidence for it, not by the reputation of the person who makes it. So the new theory about bird migration should be tested, even though it comes from an amateur.",
      o: ["A short story entered in a competition should be judged on its merits, whoever wrote it.", "Amateurs should not publish scientific papers.", "Famous scientists are usually right.", "A theory should be tested only if it is popular.", "Birdwatchers know more about birds than scientists do."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: Die **Sache** zählt, nicht der Ruf der Person. Der Wettbewerb bewertet die Geschichte selbst. Die übrigen Optionen machen den Ruf oder die Beliebtheit zum Maßstab." },
    { l: 3, q: P, p: "When resources are scarce, they should go first to those who will benefit most from them. Since the vaccine is in short supply, it should go first to the people most at risk of serious illness.",
      o: ["If there is not enough food aid for everyone, it should go first to the families who would otherwise go hungry.", "Vaccines should be given to everyone equally.", "Scarce goods should be sold to the highest bidder.", "Those who arrive first should be served first.", "People at risk should be vaccinated every year."], a: 0,
      e: "Das Prinzip: Bei **Knappheit** zuerst dorthin, wo der Nutzen am größten ist. Die Nahrungshilfe für Familien, die sonst hungern, folgt dem. Gleichverteilung, Höchstgebot und Reihenfolge des Eintreffens sind andere Verteilungsregeln." },
  ];
})();
